#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""issue-pr-reconcile — close a `pr-open` issue once its fix PR merges.

`issue_link_pr` moves an issue to `pr-open` and its own docstring says the state is
non-terminal: "the issue stays open until the PR merges, when you issue_resolve it
fixed." Nothing did that second half. No job polled linked PRs, so an issue whose fix
shipped stayed `pr-open` forever and kept counting as active work.

Measured 2026-07-30, which is why this exists: of 59 active issues, 39 were `pr-open`
and **14 of those had already-merged PRs**. The queue read as a growing backlog while
the crew was in fact draining it, and the number the operator reads each morning drifted
further from the truth every night.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, deliberately:

  - A PR closed WITHOUT merging is reported, never resolved. The fix was abandoned,
    so the issue is live work again — but deciding that is a judgment call about why
    it was abandoned, and a sweep that silently reopened or silently closed those
    would be wrong in opposite directions. It surfaces them instead.
  - An issue whose PR reference cannot be resolved is skipped and reported, never
    guessed at. See the repo-resolution note below.
  - AN EPIC IS NEVER RESOLVED ON A MERGED PR, only reported. An epic is delivered in
    slices, and the first slice to merge would otherwise close the whole thing:
    `issue_link_pr` is the only way to record which PR is doing the work, so an agent
    shipping step 1 of 5 links it to the epic and this sweep reads that as done. An
    epic's completion is a judgment against its `definition_of_done`, not a fact about
    one PR — nothing here can make that call, so it hands it to whoever can.

    Found live on 2026-08-25, before it fired: `issue_01M0N82N1PE8KEHY91HM8FCGTW` (the
    Bosun's five-step failure-detection epic) sat `pr-open` against a merged step-1 PR,
    and was the ONLY `pr-open` item in the queue — so that night's pass would have
    resolved exactly one issue and been wrong about it. The false close is also the
    expensive direction: nothing looks at a terminal state again, so an epic closed on
    its first slice loses the other four silently.
  - A MERGED PR ON A BLOCKED ITEM IS NEVER RESOLVED, only reported. `blocked` and
    its `prerequisites` say whether the item is actually done; a merged PR is not
    that answer. Found live on 2026-10-07 at 12:30Z:
    `issue_01M3BFJFKDCYPP4417RGZC75FJ` (Deck's run-register item) was resolved
    fixed because convivy/convivy-deck#326 merged, while it read `blocked: true`
    and its prerequisite — the run register's docs_given route — had not been
    built. The false close hid the missing route from everyone until Deck
    investigated.

REPO RESOLUTION — the one way this could silently corrupt the queue. A product can
span several repos (`selector` covers convivy/selector AND convivy/selector-backend),
so `product + pr` resolves to the wrong repo and would mark live work fixed against a
coincidentally-numbered PR. `mcp-ops-server.issue_link_pr` states the rule: use
`pr_url`, or `pr_repo` + `pr`, never `product + pr`; when both are absent the
reference is unresolvable, so skip. This tool obeys that and pins it with a test.

`pr_repo` is server-derived and org-pinned (the parse accepts only a convivy PR URL),
which is why it is preferred over `pr_url` — the latter is stored as supplied, as the
audit trail of what was claimed. Both are checked against the convivy org here anyway,
so a malformed or out-of-org reference skips rather than reaching `gh`.

THE THREE CASES ABOVE THAT USED TO ONLY REPORT NOW ALSO CALL THE LIBRARY, since
convivy/library#116 gave `pr-open` a route back out: `POST /items/{id}/reconcile-pr`
(`issue_client.reconcile_pr`), called with the outcome this sweep just observed —
`"merged"` for an epic whose slice merged, `"closed"` for a PR closed without
merging, `"abandoned"` for a PR still open. The route decides nothing itself; it only
knows what each ending does to the item, and that matches the refusals above exactly:
`merged`/`closed` return the item to `open` (never to a terminal resolution, so an
epic is still never resolved here), and `abandoned` moves nothing — the item stays
`pr-open` — and only raises its `needs` to `operator` so a human sees a PR that still
looks live. A route call is skipped entirely in `--dry-run`, matching every other
write this tool makes; a non-2xx from it is reported (`reconcile-failed`), never
swallowed.

Usage:
    issue-pr-reconcile              # reconcile, writing to the queue
    issue-pr-reconcile --dry-run    # report what it would do, write nothing
    issue-pr-reconcile --product X  # narrow to one product

Exits 0 on a clean pass (including a pass with nothing to do), 1 if the queue could
not be read. An unreadable queue is a failure, not an empty queue — the ARCH-311
silent no-op is exactly the class of bug this tool was written to end.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))

import issue_client as ic  # noqa: E402
import safe_path as SP  # noqa: E402
from signal_client import post_alert, sanitise  # noqa: E402

# `safe_which` never reads PATH: it checks a fixed list of system
# directories (gh resolves from `/opt/homebrew/bin` there today), so there is
# no PATH for a dispatched agent to plant a `gh` onto — the running account's
# home and a worktree are never on that list (platform/decisions/0090's
# Handoff). GH_BIN overrides it for tests. No bare "gh" fallback: that would
# hand subprocess the unfiltered PATH to search instead, reopening exactly
# the gap this routes around. None here means main() fails closed below.
GH_BIN = os.environ.get("GH_BIN") or SP.safe_which("gh")

# Accept only a convivy PR URL. Anything else is unresolvable rather than a hint —
# `pr_url` is stored as supplied, so it is attacker-influenced in a way `pr_repo`
# is not, and a permissive parse here is what would let a crafted URL point `gh` at
# an arbitrary repo.
_PR_URL_RE = re.compile(
    r"^https://github\.com/(convivy/[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*)/pull/(\d+)/?$")
# The repo name must START alphanumeric. A leading hyphen is not reachable today
# (`pr_repo` is server-derived and GitHub rejects such names), but a name that can
# be mistaken for a flag has no business being built into an argv, and the cost of
# excluding it is one character class.
_REPO_RE = re.compile(r"^convivy/[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")


def pr_ref(issue: dict[str, Any]) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """(repo, number) for an issue's fix PR, or None when unresolvable.

    Prefers the server-derived repo over the as-supplied url. Never falls back to
    `product` — see the module docstring.

    TWO SHAPES, because the two clients disagree and both reach this function. The
    loopback `/api/issues` returns the stored `refs` dict (`{url, pr, repo}`), while
    `mcp-ops-server._shape_issue` flattens the same values to `pr_url` / `pr_repo` /
    `pr` at the top level. Reading only the flattened shape is a silent no-op against
    the loopback client — every issue reads as unresolvable and the sweep reports a
    clean pass having done nothing, which is precisely how this was caught (a live
    dry run said "39 unresolvable" while all 39 carried a perfectly good `refs`).
    """
    refs = issue.get("refs")
    refs = refs if isinstance(refs, dict) else {}
    repo = issue.get("pr_repo") or refs.get("repo")
    num = issue.get("pr") or refs.get("pr")
    if isinstance(repo, str) and _REPO_RE.match(repo) and str(num or "").isdigit():
        return repo, str(num)
    url = issue.get("pr_url") or refs.get("url")
    if isinstance(url, str):
        m = _PR_URL_RE.match(url.strip())
        if m:
            return m.group(1), m.group(2)
    return None


def prerequisite_text(prerequisites: Any) -> str:
    """Render an item's `prerequisites` for a report line.

    Each entry is `{"item": "<id>"}` or `{"text": "<condition>"}` (ADR-0074).
    Both shapes are written by whoever filed or corrected the item, not a
    platform-derived value, and nothing in this repo constrains either one's
    content — so both are sanitised the same way a title is before either
    reaches a report or an alert.
    """
    if not isinstance(prerequisites, list) or not prerequisites:
        return "(none recorded)"
    parts = []
    for p in prerequisites:
        if not isinstance(p, dict):
            continue
        if p.get("text"):
            parts.append(sanitise(str(p["text"]), max_len=70))
        elif p.get("item"):
            parts.append(f"item {sanitise(str(p['item']), max_len=70)}")
    return "; ".join(parts) if parts else "(none recorded)"


def pr_state(repo: str, number: str) -> str | None:
    """"MERGED" | "CLOSED" | "OPEN", or None when GitHub could not be asked.

    None is distinct from every real state on purpose: a `gh` failure must not read
    as "not merged" (which would silently do nothing forever) nor as "merged" (which
    would close live issues). The caller reports it and moves on.
    """
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            [GH_BIN, "pr", "view", number, "--repo", repo, "--json", "state"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        state = json.loads(out.stdout).get("state")
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None
    return state if isinstance(state, str) else None


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Close pr-open issues whose fix PR merged.")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help="report what would change; write nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--product", default=None, help="narrow to one product")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if not GH_BIN:
        print("issue-pr-reconcile: no safe gh found (set GH_BIN, or put gh "
              "in a directory safe_which checks)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        issues = ic.list_issues_by_state("pr-open", product=args.product)
    except ic.IssueQueueUnavailable as e:
        print(f"issue-pr-reconcile: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    resolved: list[str] = []
    abandoned: list[str] = []   # PR closed without merging — live work again
    unresolvable: list[str] = []
    unreachable: list[str] = []
    # Kept apart from `unreachable` deliberately. A gh failure is transient and
    # self-healing, so it stays quiet. A write that failed AFTER GitHub confirmed
    # the merge means the queue is knowably wrong and only a log file says so —
    # different class, so it alerts.
    write_failed: list[str] = []
    epics: list[str] = []       # merged PR, but an epic is not closed by one slice
    epic_keys: list[str] = []   # one dedup key per (epic, merged PR) — see the alert
    blocked_merged: list[str] = []      # merged PR, but the item is blocked — prerequisites unmet
    blocked_merged_keys: list[str] = []  # one dedup key per (item, merged PR) — see the alert
    still_open = 0
    # Failures of the NEW `reconcile_pr` calls below, kept apart from `write_failed`
    # (which is scoped to the ordinary `resolve_issue` write) because the alert text
    # for each says something different about what is now wrong with the queue.
    reconcile_failed: list[str] = []

    for issue in issues:
        iid = issue.get("id") or "?"
        product = issue.get("product") or "?"
        # An issue title is free text authored by agents (or anyone with queue
        # access), not a platform-authored value, so it is sanitised before it
        # reaches the console rows or the alert body. Same rule as every other
        # API response body — see signal_client.sanitise. The 70-char cap rides
        # on max_len so the sanitiser sees the full title first: truncating
        # first would leave a bisected credential value too short for the
        # redaction regexes to match.
        title = sanitise(issue.get("title") or "", max_len=70)
        ref = pr_ref(issue)
        if ref is None:
            unresolvable.append(f"{iid} [{product}] {title}")
            continue
        repo, number = ref
        state = pr_state(repo, number)
        if state is None:
            unreachable.append(f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number}")
            continue
        if state == "OPEN":
            still_open += 1
            # The PR still looks live, so the item must not move on its own —
            # `reconcile_pr("abandoned")` moves nothing either; it only raises
            # `needs` to `operator` so a human sees it. Never derived as a
            # judgment here: the sweep reports what `gh` said and lets the route's
            # own conservative handling decide what, if anything, changes.
            if not args.dry_run:
                res = ic.reconcile_pr(iid, "abandoned")
                if res.get("status") != "ok":
                    reconcile_failed.append(
                        f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — reconcile(abandoned) "
                        f"failed: {res.get('status')}")
            continue
        if state == "CLOSED":
            abandoned.append(f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — {title}")
            if not args.dry_run:
                res = ic.reconcile_pr(iid, "closed")
                if res.get("status") != "ok":
                    reconcile_failed.append(
                        f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — reconcile(closed) "
                        f"failed: {res.get('status')}")
            continue
        # MERGED
        url = f"https://github.com/{repo}/pull/{number}"
        line = f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — {title}"
        # An epic is delivered in slices; the first one to merge must not close it.
        # Reported rather than skipped silently, because a `pr-open` epic whose PR
        # has merged is a real state somebody has to act on — the epic is either
        # done, or its next slice needs linking — and this sweep is the only thing
        # that looks. `reconcile_pr("merged")` unsticks it (pr-open → open) without
        # resolving it, so it stays claimable; the epic itself is still never
        # resolved here. An epic that is ALSO blocked takes this path, not the
        # blocked one below, because the check runs first and the per-slice
        # hold is a separate judgment from `blocked` (ADR-0074). Either report
        # says a human has to look; this just picks the one that fits the item.
        if (issue.get("type") or "") == "epic":
            epics.append(line)
            epic_keys.append(f"reconcile-epic-held:{iid}:{repo}#{number}")
            if not args.dry_run:
                res = ic.reconcile_pr(iid, "merged")
                if res.get("status") != "ok":
                    reconcile_failed.append(
                        f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — reconcile(merged) "
                        f"failed: {res.get('status')}")
            continue
        # A blocked item's PR merging does not mean the item is done — `blocked`
        # and `prerequisites` are what decide that, and a merged PR is not that
        # answer (see the module docstring's 2026-10-07 incident). Reported and
        # unstuck via `reconcile_pr("merged")`, exactly like the epic hold above,
        # never resolved here.
        if issue.get("blocked"):
            prereqs = prerequisite_text(issue.get("prerequisites"))
            blocked_merged.append(f"{line} — prerequisites unmet: {prereqs}")
            blocked_merged_keys.append(f"reconcile-blocked-merged-held:{iid}:{repo}#{number}")
            if not args.dry_run:
                res = ic.reconcile_pr(iid, "merged")
                if res.get("status") != "ok":
                    reconcile_failed.append(
                        f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — reconcile(merged) "
                        f"failed: {res.get('status')}")
            continue
        if args.dry_run:
            resolved.append(f"[dry-run] {line}")
            continue
        res = ic.resolve_issue(
            iid, "fixed",
            outcome_note=f"Fix PR {repo}#{number} merged; reconciled by issue-pr-reconcile.",
            fix_pr=url)
        if res.get("status") == "ok":
            resolved.append(line)
        else:
            write_failed.append(f"{iid} [{product}] {repo}#{number} — resolve failed: "
                                f"{res.get('status')}")

    for label, rows in (("resolved", resolved), ("PR closed unmerged — live work again", abandoned),
                        ("epic with a merged PR — NOT closed, needs a human call",
                         epics),
                        ("blocked item with a merged PR — NOT resolved, "
                         "prerequisites unmet", blocked_merged),
                        ("unresolvable PR reference — skipped", unresolvable),
                        ("merged, but the resolve write failed", write_failed),
                        ("PR outcome recorded, but the reconcile-pr write failed",
                         reconcile_failed),
                        ("could not check — skipped", unreachable)):
        if rows:
            print(f"issue-pr-reconcile: {len(rows)} {label}:")
            for r in rows:
                print(f"    {r}")

    print(f"issue-pr-reconcile: {len(issues)} pr-open, {len(resolved)} resolved, "
          f"{still_open} PR still open, {len(abandoned)} abandoned, "
          f"{len(epics)} epic(s) held, {len(blocked_merged)} blocked item(s) held, "
          f"{len(unresolvable)} unresolvable, {len(write_failed)} write-failed, "
          f"{len(reconcile_failed)} reconcile-failed, "
          f"{len(unreachable)} unreachable")

    # Only the states that need a human decision, or that leave the queue knowably
    # wrong, raise an alert. A merged PR closing its issue is the normal path and
    # stays quiet; a routine sweep that alerted every night would train the alerts
    # page to be ignored, which costs the alerts that matter.
    # THE EPIC ALERT IS SEPARATE AND DEDUPED, because it is the only one of these
    # that is an EXPECTED, LONG-LIVED state rather than an exception. An epic sits
    # between slices for days or weeks, and the combined alert below carries no
    # suppression — so folding epics into it would post the same body every night
    # until a human acted, which is precisely what the comment under it warns
    # trains the alerts page to be ignored. That would cost the alerts that matter,
    # and this one is not safe to ignore.
    #
    # Keyed on the epic AND the merged PR, so the alert fires once on the
    # TRANSITION — an epic's slice merged, it now needs a call — and not again
    # while nothing has changed. The next slice's PR is a new key, so it alerts
    # again then. The nightly report block above still lists every held epic, so
    # the standing state stays visible without being re-announced.
    if not args.dry_run and epics:
        for line, key in zip(epics, epic_keys):
            post_alert("agent-platform",
                       "An epic's fix PR merged and the epic was deliberately NOT closed. "
                       "An epic ships in slices, so one merged PR does not mean done — "
                       "check its definition_of_done, then either resolve it or link the "
                       f"next slice's PR.\n\n  {line}",
                       dedup_key=key)

    # Deduped the same way as the epic alert above, on the item AND the merged PR,
    # keyed separately so one alert's dedup never suppresses the other — a nightly
    # re-run must not repeat this once it has fired for a given transition, but a
    # different merged PR on the same still-blocked item is a new transition and
    # alerts again.
    if not args.dry_run and blocked_merged:
        for line, key in zip(blocked_merged, blocked_merged_keys):
            post_alert("agent-platform",
                       "A blocked item's fix PR merged while its prerequisites are unmet. "
                       "A merged PR does not finish an item whose prerequisites are "
                       "unmet — check them, then either resolve the item or link the PR "
                       f"that completes them.\n\n  {line}",
                       dedup_key=key)

    if not args.dry_run and (abandoned or unresolvable or write_failed or reconcile_failed):
        body = ["issue-pr-reconcile found issues needing a human call.", ""]
        if abandoned:
            body.append(f"{len(abandoned)} PR(s) closed without merging — the fix was "
                        "abandoned, so the issue is live work again:")
            body.extend(f"  {r}" for r in abandoned)
            body.append("")
        if unresolvable:
            body.append(f"{len(unresolvable)} issue(s) carry a PR reference that cannot be "
                        "resolved to a convivy repo, so merged state is unknowable:")
            body.extend(f"  {r}" for r in unresolvable)
            body.append("")
        if write_failed:
            body.append(f"{len(write_failed)} issue(s) have a CONFIRMED-merged PR whose "
                        "resolve write failed. The queue is overcounting these until the "
                        "next pass retries; a repeat across passes means the write path "
                        "is broken rather than flaky:")
            body.extend(f"  {r}" for r in write_failed)
            body.append("")
        if reconcile_failed:
            body.append(f"{len(reconcile_failed)} issue(s) have a PR outcome this sweep "
                        "observed, but the Library's reconcile-pr write failed to record "
                        "it — the item may still be stuck in pr-open, or missing its "
                        "operator flag, until the next pass retries; a repeat across "
                        "passes means the write path is broken rather than flaky:")
            body.extend(f"  {r}" for r in reconcile_failed)
        post_alert("agent-platform", "\n".join(body))

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
